course: dictionary
lesson: 5313
title: Keen: many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Keen is one of those words, so the words around it tell you which meaning to pick. Read the clues before you choose.
explain: When keen means eager or interested, it often has «on» or «to» after it: «She is keen on football.» In everyday UK, Irish, Australian and NZ speech, «Are you keen?» asks if you want to do something, such as come along.
explain: When keen means sharp or strong, it describes senses or weather: «keen eyesight» and «a keen wind». Ask yourself which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence to check.
fact: Keen is an adjective, and it can change to keener and keenest.
fact: «Keen» means very eager or interested.
fact: «Keen» can also mean sharp or strong, as in «keen eyesight».
fact: «Keen to» means wanting to do something, so «Are you keen?» means do you want to come?
fact: Keen means eager, so «not keen» means you do not want to do something.
exercise: «She is keen on football.» What does keen mean here? | Sharp and cold | *Very interested in it | Lost | Tired of it
exercise: «The eagle has keen eyesight.» What does keen mean here? | Eager to fly | Very old | *Sharp and strong | Quiet
exercise: Sam asks, «Are you keen?» What does he mean? | *Do you want to come? | Are you lost? | Are you sharp at maths? | Are you very old?
test: «Letting little ones win sometimes keeps them keen.» What does keen mean here? | *Eager to keep playing | Lost | Very old | Sharp and cold
test: Mia says, «I'm not keen on spiders.» What does not keen mean? | *She does not want to be near them | She can see them very sharply | She is asleep | She is very interested in them
exam: «A keen wind blew across the hill.» What does keen mean here? | Eager to go | Full of sand | Very quiet | *Sharp and cold
